Four things break in your app on .NET 11:

---

### 1. GZipStream empty-payload check breaks
GZipStream now **always writes headers/footers**, even with no data. Your zero-byte length check will never trigger.

**Fix:** Track the uncompressed byte count instead of checking output length:
```csharp
// Before (.NET 10): ms.Length == 0 meant "no data"
// After (.NET 11): ms.Length > 0 even with no data written

// Fix: track whether you actually wrote anything
bool wroteData = false;
using (var gz = new GZipStream(ms, CompressionMode.Compress, leaveOpen: true))
{
    if (hasData) { gz.Write(data); wroteData = true; }
}
if (!wroteData) { /* handle "no data" */ }
```

---

### 2. TAR header checksum validation now enforced
TAR-reading APIs **verify checksums** on read. Your hand-crafted/legacy TAR files with invalid checksums will now **throw exceptions**.

**Fix:** Add error handling, or fix the legacy tool's output:
```csharp
try
{
    using var reader = new TarReader(stream);
    var entry = reader.GetNextEntry();
}
catch (InvalidDataException ex)
{
    // Handle legacy TAR files with bad checksums
}
```

---

### 3. ZipArchive.CreateAsync now eagerly loads entries
Previously lazy-loaded entries are now **eagerly loaded into memory**. For your large ZIP files, this may cause **significant memory pressure** or `OutOfMemoryException`.

**Fix:** Monitor memory usage. If problematic, consider processing entries in smaller batches or using the synchronous `ZipArchive` constructor which may still use lazy loading.

---

### 4. MemoryStream maximum capacity & exception behavior changed
The maximum capacity has been updated and the **exception types** thrown when exceeding capacity have changed. Code catching specific exceptions near max capacity will break.

**Fix:** Review your large-allocation code — update any `catch` blocks to handle the new exception behavior, and verify your allocations still fit within the updated limits.

---

**Summary:** Items 1 and 2 are most likely to cause immediate failures. Item 3 is a memory concern for large archives. Item 4 requires reviewing your exception-handling logic around large `MemoryStream` allocations.
